    {
      "time": 1784934318.9205377,
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "df.groupby(\"Country\")[\"Churned\"].mean().sort_values()\n\ncan u explain this code? how is it able to give curn rate per country?",
      "id": "666df0b1-7ddd-4291-8478-bf691d409d5a",
      "raw_time": false,
      "sender": "72495e6509da46ca9f01ee7f4d0258f4"
    },
    {
      "time": 1784934327.7963595,
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::OpenCodeAcpPersona",
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "## Step-by-Step Explanation\n\n| Code Part | What It Does |\n|-----------|--------------|\n| `df.groupby(\"Country\")` | Groups all rows by unique country values |\n| `[\"Churned\"]` | Selects only the Churned column (0 or 1) |\n| `.mean()` | Calculates average of 0s and 1s per group |\n| `.sort_values()` | Sorts from lowest to highest churn rate |\n\n## Why `.mean()` Gives Churn Rate\n\n`Churned` is binary: **0 = stayed**, **1 = churned**\n\nSo mean = `(0 + 1 + 1 + 0 + ...) / total` = **proportion who churned** = **churn rate**\n\n**Example:**\n- Country A: [0, 0, 1, 1, 1] \u2192 mean = 3/5 = **0.6 (60% churn)**\n- Country B: [0, 0, 0, 1, 0] \u2192 mean = 1/5 = **0.2 (20% churn)**\n\nThat's why the output directly shows churn rate per country!",
      "id": "d8a7af8e-f3b3-4be7-bf4f-b78769add0f5",
      "mentions": [],
      "raw_time": false
    },
